From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shipwright <shipwright@invalid>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 22:43:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Expose hearing features that need Apple's Device ID

AirPods offer hearing aid control, transparency mode customisation and
loud sound reduction only to hosts whose Device ID carries Apple's
company identifier (0x004C). This adds them as capabilities, in the
existing SetCapabilities/info style:

- hearingAid: {selected, enrolled}. AAP control commands 0x2C
  (enrolled, enabled) and 0x33 (hearing assist). Turning it on without
  an audiogram on the headphones is refused.
- hearingAidAdjustments: amplification, balance, tone, ambient noise
  reduction, conversation boost. ATT attribute 0x2A; the audiogram in
  the same attribute is kept.
- transparencyCustom: the same adjustments plus on/off for transparency
  mode. ATT attribute 0x18.
- loudSoundReduction: {selected}. ATT attribute 0x1B, read back after
  each write since it does not notify.

Without Apple's Device ID (see HostDeviceId) each capability that the
model has is listed as {"unavailable": "needs Apple Device ID",
"readonly": true} while the headphones are connected, and
SetCapabilities ignores it, so clients can explain why a feature is
missing.

The ATT attributes are reached through a small ATT client on the
BR/EDR L2CAP channel PSM 0x1F (AapAttChannel). It runs while the AAP
link is up and the host presents Apple's Device ID, sends one request
at a time, answers MTU exchanges and other requests from the headphones,
and confirms indications. A response is taken only if it answers the
request in flight (Read Response for a read, Write Response for a write,
an Error Response naming the request's opcode and handle). Read
Responses carry no handle, so when a request gets no answer within 3 s
(checked from its deadline, not when the next request is queued) the
channel is closed, as ATT requires after a transaction timeout, every
pending request fails, and the device reconnects it (at most three times
per AAP connection). Start and Stop are serialised, and the connecting
thread re-checks that the channel is still wanted before and after
connecting.

Values are validated per attribute before they are used: transparency
100 or 104+ bytes with a 0.0/1.0 flag, hearing aid 104+ bytes with
0x60/0x64 in the header, loud sound reduction exactly one byte 0/1, and
finite floats. A write starts from the value as last read and rewrites
only the fields in the request, so the audiogram, ears set differently
and any bytes a newer firmware adds are kept.

Model support follows LibrePods: hearing aid and loud sound reduction on
AirPods Pro 2 (Lightning, USB-C) and Pro 3; transparency customisation
on the models with a transparency mode.

Protocol reference: LibrePods (https://github.com/librepods-org/librepods):
docs/control_commands.md, android ATTManager.kt, Transparency.kt,
HearingAid.kt, linux/hearing-aid-adjustments.py. Codec and channel
tests (with a fake transport) are in TestsAapAppleGated (DEBUG builds).
