+### Features that need Apple's Device ID
+
+AirPods offer some features only to hosts whose Device ID (the DID profile)
+carries Apple's Bluetooth SIG company identifier, 0x004C. With BlueZ, set
+`DeviceID = bluetooth:004C:0000:0000` in the `[General]` section of
+`/etc/bluetooth/main.conf` and restart `bluetooth`; you may have to pair the
+headphones again, because they cache the host's Device ID. Every Bluetooth
+device near the host then sees it as an Apple device. `GetHostDevice`
+says whether the adapter presents Apple's Device ID.
+
+These capabilities are listed only for models that have the feature, and only
+while the headphones are connected. Without Apple's Device ID they are listed
+as
+
+```json
+{
+  "hearingAid": {
+    "readonly": true,
+    "unavailable": "needs Apple Device ID"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+and `SetCapabilities` ignores them. With it, they are listed once the
+headphones report their state. The hearing features are attributes on an ATT
+channel (L2CAP PSM 0x1F) that the daemon opens next to the AAP channel.
+Protocol details: [LibrePods](https://github.com/librepods-org/librepods)
+(`docs/control_commands.md`, the Android app's `ATTManager.kt`,
+`Transparency.kt` and `HearingAid.kt`, `linux/hearing-aid-adjustments.py`).
+
+##### Hearing aid
+
+AirPods Pro 2 and later. Turns the hearing aid on or off. `enrolled` is `true`
+when the headphones hold an audiogram; turning the hearing aid on without one
+is refused. The audiogram itself cannot be set through this API.
+
+```json
+{
+  "hearingAid": {
+    "readonly": false,
+    "selected": true,
+    "enrolled": true
+  }
+}
+```
+
+##### Hearing aid adjustments
+
+AirPods Pro 2 and later. Fine tuning of the hearing aid. Any subset of the
+fields may be set, and only those are written: the audiogram stored in the
+same attribute, and every field not in the request, are kept exactly as the
+headphones reported them. Settings are refused until the attribute has been
+read.
+
+```json
+{
+  "hearingAidAdjustments": {
+    "readonly": false,
+    "amplification": 0,
+    "balance": 0,
+    "tone": 0,
+    "ambientNoiseReduction": 0,
+    "conversationBoost": false
+  }
+}
+```
+
+| Field                   |                                          |
+| ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
+| `amplification`         | `-100..100`                              |
+| `balance`               | `-100..100`, negative left, positive right |
+| `tone`                  | `-100..100`, negative darker, positive brighter |
+| `ambientNoiseReduction` | `0..100`                                 |
+| `conversationBoost`     | bool                                     |
+
+The two ears get `amplification - balance / 2` and
+`amplification + balance / 2`; setting only one of the two keeps the other
+as the ears have it. `tone`, `ambientNoiseReduction` and `conversationBoost`
+set both ears. The values read are those of the left ear for these three,
+and the mean and difference of the two ears for amplification and balance,
+in whole percent; ears set differently (for example from an iPhone) keep
+their difference until one of these fields is set.
+
+##### Transparency mode customisation
+
+AirPods Pro, AirPods 4 with ANC, AirPods Max. `selected` turns the custom
+transparency settings on; the other fields are those of hearing aid
+adjustments. Not used while the hearing aid is on.
+
+```json
+{
+  "transparencyCustom": {
+    "readonly": false,
+    "selected": true,
+    "amplification": 20,
+    "balance": 0,
+    "tone": 0,
+    "ambientNoiseReduction": 50,
+    "conversationBoost": true
+  }
+}
+```
+
+##### Loud sound reduction
+
+AirPods Pro 2 and later. Reduces loud environmental sound in transparency and
+adaptive modes. The headphones do not notify changes of this attribute, so the
+daemon reads it back after every write.
+
+```json
+{
+  "loudSoundReduction": {
+    "readonly": false,
+    "selected": true
+  }
+}
+```
+
+
 ## Settings
 
 A general settings storage. Settings are stored in containers in TOML format. Container names must use only Latin characters; the names must not include magicpods or MAC addresses. Allowed separators are _, -, or camelCase. Supported types are bool, string, int, and float.
